// src/v4/extdeps/languages/cpp.dag
//
// Scope: C++ language model — direction-agnostic. Subsumes C subset
//        (C is a syntactic subset of C++).
// Anchor: ISO/IEC 14882, C++ standard — the versioned upstream
//         specification (L-2 fidelity authority).
//         https://www.iso.org/standard/79358.html
//         cppreference is a navigation aid, not the authority.
// Unifies: (per THESIS:188) language spec = transport spec =
//         interpreter runtime. ONE substrate carrier for all three
//         roles. Different lenses read different facts from the same
//         data (emission reads syntax; transport reads serialization;
//         interpreter reads execution semantics). A worker needing
//         transport or runtime extends this file, not a new "transport
//         spec" file.
//
// Owns:
//   - C++ grammar (declarations, expressions, statements, templates, classes)
//   - Type system (primitive types + struct + union + class + template instantiation)
//   - D2 resolver facts for C++ primitive carriers: alias-identity to
//     std/ carriers and operation-semantics carriers. This file is NOT
//     an algebra-inhabitance site; the std/ scalar files own algebra
//     once. D2a(2) grounding-map rows are tracked-scaffold deferred
//     because the shared `GroundingMap` home is operator-pending.
//   - Cost realization: per-operation cost on C++ targets
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/logic.dag: Bool (kernel-ambient; alias-identity below)
//   - the std/ scalar files (integer/float/text) when their C++ alias
//     targets land. No std/algebra.dag import in this slice because
//     D2 forbids per-language algebra re-declaration.
//
// Status: T-4 cpp D2-resolver modeled 2026-05-16 (sunny-cat-482).
//   Mirrors the canonical rust.dag D2-resolver DISCIPLINE from PR
//   #3174 at 0328bee9: resolver facts only, no per-language algebra
//   inhabitance, no IrToCpp string table, no grammar operation stub.
//   Header edits encode the resolver shape under DECISIONS.md D2's
//   delegated "extdeps/languages/* headers" authority.
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.languages.cpp


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes
//
// D2 RESOLVER, NOT INHABITANCE. Per DECISIONS.md D2, a language
// extdeps file is a resolver for abstract std/ carriers. It contributes
// three thin facets per primitive:
//   (1) alias-identity to the std/ carrier (`type CppBool = Bool`);
//   (2) a grounding map carrying the source spelling — DEFERRED here
//       because the shared `GroundingMap` type has no pinned home yet;
//   (3) operation-semantics carriers that are genuine C++ facts.
//
// It must NOT declare `OrderedRing<CppInt, ...>` or a sibling algebra
// instance for any C++ primitive. Integer, float, text, and boolean
// algebra inhabitance live in std/ (`std/integer.dag`,
// `std/float.dag`, `std/text.dag`, `std/logic.dag`) and flow through
// alias identity. Re-declaring them here would be the parallel
// substrate INVARIANTS P1 forbids (`numeric_aliases_align_to_refinements`).
//
// C++ IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED WIDTHS. Unlike Rust, C++'s core integer
// types (`short`, `int`, `long`, `long long`, `wchar_t`) are specified
// by constraints and implementation-defined representation choices, not
// by fixed source-level bit widths. This file therefore cannot land
// aliases such as `type CppInt = Int32` without a target ABI / data-model
// resolver fact. The correct D2 shape is still alias identity, but the
// alias target must be selected by a declared implementation model
// (LP64/LLP64/ILP32 or a future more precise target-data carrier), not
// guessed in cpp.dag.
//
// GRAMMAR FACTS OWNED ELSEWHERE. D2 covers per-primitive inhabitance
// form only. It does not settle C++ grammar ambiguity or bidirectional
// parse/emit behavior. Syntax that needs semantic feedback to parse
// (`T(a)`-class declaration-vs-expression ambiguity, most-vexing-parse,
// `<` template-argument vs less-than contexts) belongs to the T-4
// LanguageModel grammar slice and C5-3 fidelity dispositions. This file
// does not stub `emit_cpp`, `ingest_cpp`, or an `Outcome<T>` result
// shape.
//
// LIBRARIES ARE NOT THE SPEC SURFACE. `std::string`, `std::vector<T>`,
// `std::int32_t`, and friends are library/program nodes in the modeled
// language, not primitive C++ language carriers. The core-language
// primitive resolver here names only fundamental types. Fixed-width
// typedefs from `<cstdint>` can be modeled by the library/program surface
// once library ingestion is in scope; they are not core extdeps facts.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// D2a(2) grounding-map facet — 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD DEFERRED.
//
// Per the T-4 manager brief (msg_c131bdf6, 2026-05-16), `GroundingMap`
// is a SHARED resolver type with no pinned substrate home yet. Declaring
// a local `GroundingMap<IRCarrier>` in every language file would create
// N parallel declarations for one fact, violating INVARIANTS P2.
//
// Therefore this file intentionally does NOT declare:
//   - `type GroundingMap<IRCarrier> { spelling: String }`
//   - `data cpp_bool_grounding: GroundingMap<CppBool> = ...`
//   - any future `cpp_<primitive>_grounding` rows
//
// Dissolution trigger: when the operator pins the shared `GroundingMap`
// home, C++ primitive grounding rows amend in as consumers of that
// single authority. Until then D2a(1) alias-identity and D2a(3)
// operation-semantics can land without fabricating the shared type.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CppIntegerArithmeticSemantics — the closed IN-F C++ integer
// arithmetic-semantics set for core operators.
// Anchor: C++ draft [basic.fundamental] unsigned integer range and
// arithmetic modulo 2^N:
//   https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.fundamental
// Anchor: C++ draft [expr.pre] undefined behavior rule for expression
// evaluation outside mathematically-defined result range:
//   https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.pre
//
//   - RepresentableResultRequired  signed arithmetic is defined only
//                                  when the mathematical result is
//                                  representable by the result type.
//                                  If not, the source program is outside
//                                  F and the consumer must fail closed.
//   - UnsignedModuloArithmetic     unsigned arithmetic is defined modulo
//                                  2^N for the unsigned type's width.
//
// Signed integer OVERFLOW is deliberately NOT a variant here. The C++
// standard gives overflow undefined behavior; per C5-3 and the manager
// brief, that is fail-closed / out-of-F, not a third "action" inside a
// language-wide overflow taxonomy. `RepresentableResultRequired` models
// the in-F signed-operation precondition; a consumer that cannot prove it
// rejects with a Diagnostic rather than producing a fabricated semantic
// value.
//
// This is operation-semantics data, not algebra inhabitance. Unsigned
// bounded integer aliases may point at std/ `UIntN` carriers; this
// sibling disposition records C++'s defined modulo arithmetic. Signed
// aliases point at `RepresentableResultRequired`, not at an undefined-
// behavior value.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: emit, ingest, runtime-equivalence, and
//      Tier-2 safety consumers all need the same arithmetic semantics
//      fact; shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a string/Symbol tag would reopen the
//      K-1 readable-tag anti-pattern. A Bool such as
//      `{ wraps: true }` would also collapse the signed precondition
//      and unsigned modulo rule into a boolean proxy.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: unsigned modulo arithmetic is ring-like
//      over a fixed width, but the algebra lives on the std/ `UIntN`
//      carrier. This C++ carrier records the language's operation
//      disposition, not a second algebra instance.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: signed representability and unsigned modulo
//      are alternatives selected by the primitive family, not
//      simultaneous coordinates. The undefined signed-overflow case is
//      outside F, so it is absence/fail-closed, not a value carried
//      simultaneously with the in-F semantics.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants are the C++
//      standard's own signed-vs-unsigned arithmetic semantics. The type
//      parameter on `IntegerOperationSemantics<IRCarrier>` supplies the
//      concrete carrier; no richer source set collapses this variant.
// Terminal: the closed in-F C++ core integer arithmetic-semantics set.
type CppIntegerArithmeticSemantics
  = RepresentableResultRequired
  | UnsignedModuloArithmetic


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IntegerOperationSemantics<IRCarrier> — the C++ integer operation-
// semantics carrier for a primitive alias. Conj record (no coproduct
// ledger).
// Anchor: C++ draft [basic.fundamental], unsigned arithmetic modulo 2^N:
//   https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.fundamental
// Anchor: C++ draft [expr.pre], result not mathematically defined or not
// in representable range is undefined behavior:
//   https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.pre
//
//   - arithmetic_semantics  the C++ core-language arithmetic semantics
//                           for this primitive family.
//
// Signed core integer aliases use `RepresentableResultRequired`;
// overflow itself is undefined behavior and therefore fail-closed /
// out-of-F unless a proof or explicit totalized carrier makes the
// operation defined. Unsigned aliases use `UnsignedModuloArithmetic`.
// This record is deliberately a sibling of the alias, not a field on
// the std/ integer carrier, because the same abstract integer carrier
// may realize differently across languages.
type IntegerOperationSemantics<IRCarrier> {
  arithmetic_semantics: CppIntegerArithmeticSemantics
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-primitive alias-identity declarations.
//
// LANDABLE NOW — grounded against current origin/main carriers:

// C++ `bool` ↔ std/logic.dag's `Bool`.
// Anchor: C++ draft [basic.fundamental], boolean type:
//   https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.fundamental
//
// Alias-identity is the machine-readable D2 inhabitance form: CppBool
// is literally the same Node carrier as Bool, so Bool's BooleanAlgebra
// inhabitance flows through std/logic.dag's single authority.
type CppBool = Bool

// Bool has no integer overflow / NaN operation-semantics row.


// 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD — DEFERRED ALIAS-IDENTITY ROWS
//
// The rows below are not omitted decisions; they are D2-form commitments
// blocked on named upstream carriers. When the owner lands, each row
// amends in as:
//   - `type Cpp<X> = <StdCarrier>` alias-identity;
//   - grounding row ONLY after the shared `GroundingMap` home is pinned;
//   - for integer aliases, `data cpp_<x>_integer_semantics:
//      IntegerOperationSemantics<Cpp<X>> = IntegerOperationSemantics {
//        arithmetic_semantics: <RepresentableResultRequired |
//        UnsignedModuloArithmetic> }`.
// Signed aliases carry `RepresentableResultRequired`; signed overflow
// itself is fail-closed/out-of-F unless separately proven safe or
// totalized. There is no undefined-behavior variant.
//
// SUBSTRATE SINGLE-AUTHORITY GUARD. cpp.dag MUST NOT mint local
// `Int`, `UInt`, `Float`, `Char`, or `String` carriers. The std/ scalar
// files are the sole authority. C++ supplies resolver facts and genuine
// per-language operation semantics only.
//
// C++ signed core integers — signed char / short int / int / long int /
//                            long long int
//   - target form        alias to the std signed integer carrier selected
//                        by the declared C++ implementation data model,
//                        plus a future shared grounding row and
//                        `RepresentableResultRequired` integer
//                        operation semantics.
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag for the Int/IntN ladder;
//                        target implementation model for source type →
//                        width mapping (LP64/LLP64/ILP32 or successor).
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when std/integer.dag lands the signed ladder
//                        AND the C++ target data-model resolver can pick
//                        the width without guessing.
//
// C++ unsigned core integers — unsigned char / unsigned short int /
//                              unsigned int / unsigned long int /
//                              unsigned long long int
//   - target form        alias to the std unsigned integer carrier
//                        selected by the declared implementation model,
//                        plus a future shared grounding row and
//                        `UnsignedModuloArithmetic` integer operation
//                        semantics.
//   - named owner        std/integer.dag for the UInt/UIntN ladder;
//                        target implementation model for widths.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        same as signed, with unsigned carrier targets.
//
// C++ character types — char / signed char / unsigned char / wchar_t /
//                       char8_t / char16_t / char32_t
//   - target form        char8_t/char16_t/char32_t are code-unit types,
//                        not automatically std/text.dag `Char` (Unicode
//                        scalar value). `char` / `wchar_t` are
//                        implementation-defined code-unit carriers.
//   - named owner        std/text.dag refined code-point / code-unit
//                        carriers plus the C++ implementation model.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when text.dag exposes the needed code-unit and
//                        Unicode-scalar refinements, or an operator-
//                        ratified per-language refinement-disposition
//                        carrier decides where the refinement lives.
//
// C++ floating types — float / double / long double
//   - target form        alias to std/float.dag carriers selected by the
//                        implementation's floating format facts, plus
//                        grounding and IEEE / implementation-specific
//                        NaN/Inf disposition rows.
//   - named owner        std/float.dag for Float32/Float64 and the
//                        ApproximateField inhabitance; target
//                        implementation model for `long double` and
//                        non-IEC-559 implementations.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when std/float.dag lands its Float ladder and
//                        the C++ implementation model declares the
//                        concrete formats without guessing.
//
// C++ `void` — no value carrier.
//   - target form        not an alias to Bool/Int/Float/Text. `void`
//                        is the absence of a value in function return
//                        position, modeled with the function / type
//                        substrate when the grammar slice lands.
//   - named owner        T-4 LanguageModel grammar + std/cardinality
//                        empty/unit carrier decision.
//   - dissolution trigger
//                        when function return-shape modeling needs a
//                        machine-readable `void` carrier.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// D2b — C++ floating-operation realizations.
//
// For irreducible floating arithmetic, DECISIONS.md D2b uses the same
// single external-primitive path as every language: `Transform →
// FunctionRef → Arrow`, where the Arrow's body is an extdeps realization.
// C++ operator spellings, precedence, overload-resolution boundaries,
// and NaN/Inf handling are LanguageModel / operation-realization data.
//
// This slice declares the resolver carriers they will use; it does not
// author the Arrow-body realizations before std/float.dag's
// ApproximateField instance and the T-4 grammar production data exist.
